    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Discovering Nature's Wonders – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Nature’s Wonders</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Rainforest.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify familiar vocabulary from daily life used in context. Interactive: Engage in short exchanges using phrases learned from classroom discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify and respond accurately to familiar vocabulary from daily life when used in discussions (e.g., "What animal is slow?" "The sloth."). Can engage with familiar phrases about animals and nature (e.g., "Where does the sloth live?" "It lives in the rainforest."). Can demonstrate comprehension of numbers or quantities associated with familiar topics when spoken slowly and clearly (e.g., "three fish" or "five trees").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and plays a short, mysterious jungle soundscape. The teacher then shows a large, colorful picture of a rainforest. Teacher asks, "What do you see?" and points to animals/elements. Students respond with sounds or gestures, or single words like "tree," "animal." (Listening for sounds, visual recognition).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces flashcards of key rainforest animals one by one (sloth, toucan, monkey, parrot, frog). For each card, the teacher says the word clearly, modeling pronunciation. "This is a sloth." (Pronunciation focus: /s/ in "sloth"). "This is a rainforest." (Pronunciation focus: /r/ in "rainforest"). "This is a jungle." (Phonemic awareness: J /dʒ/ "jungle"). Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher acts out simple characteristics (e.g., slow for sloth, jumping for frog) using TPR.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary. </p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a sloth fast or slow?" (Students listen and point to "slow" flashcard or show a slow movement).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a cat or a monkey?" (Teacher shows monkey flashcard. Students listen and say "monkey" or point to the monkey).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short narrative audio story (teacher's voice or simple recording) about a visit to the rainforest, accompanied by large picture flashcards/illustrations of the target vocabulary (rainforest, jungle, sloth, toucan, monkey, parrot, frog, leaf, river, waterfall). The story is simple, using short sentences and present simple tense. E.g., "This is the rainforest. It's green. A sloth lives here. The sloth is slow and furry. A colorful toucan flies in the trees. Monkeys climb and swing. They are jumpy. A frog sits on a leaf."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identify familiar vocabulary from daily life used in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening focus):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> After listening to the whole story, the teacher asks, "Where are we?" (Students listen and point to the rainforest picture or say "rainforest").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher plays the story again, pausing after each key animal or feature is mentioned. Students listen and point to the corresponding flashcard or picture on a large rainforest poster. (e.g., "A sloth lives here." - students point to the sloth).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Count:</b> The teacher slowly says phrases like "three trees" or "two colorful parrots". Students listen and show the correct number using their fingers, or point to images and count them.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying animals and their characteristics through listening. They will listen for adjectives + nouns (e.g., "slow sloth," "colorful toucan," "jumpy monkey") and respond to simple WH-questions (e.g., "What animal is it?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Match (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with pictures of rainforest animals (sloth, toucan, monkey, frog) and separate word labels for their names and adjectives (slow, furry, colorful, jumpy). The teacher says a sentence, e.g., "It's a slow sloth." Students listen and draw a line from the sentence or description word to the correct animal picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Circle (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher says a characteristic or a short phrase, and students listen and circle the correct animal on their worksheet. E.g., Teacher: "Listen: The animal is green and jumps." Students circle the frog. Teacher: "Listen: What animal is furry and slow?" Students circle the sloth. This reinforces listening for specific details.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Teacher Says, Students Point (Pair/Group Interaction - Listening focus):</b> Students are in small groups with a set of flashcards. The teacher says a simple descriptive phrase about an animal, e.g., "It's a colorful bird. It can fly." Students listen and collaboratively point to the toucan or parrot flashcard. The teacher uses WH-questions like "What animal is it?" to guide them, and students listen and point.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Rainforest Animal Sounds & Description Guessing Game."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a short audio clip of an animal sound (e.g., monkey screech, frog croak).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After the sound, the teacher gives a simple verbal description using learned vocabulary and grammar: "It is jumpy. It swings in the trees." or "It is green. It lives in the river." (Pragmatic: Describing animals with traits).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to both the sound and the description.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then point to the correct animal flashcard or picture on a large chart, or raise a card with the animal's picture. The teacher asks "What animal is it?" and students listen to their peers' answers or point. (Sociolinguistic: Talking about the rainforest and animals using respectful language implicitly by correct identification).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This task focuses on listening comprehension for both auditory cues (sounds) and verbal descriptions to identify familiar vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Rainforest Animal Sounds & Description Guessing Game." The teacher observes students' ability to listen and accurately identify the animals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher presents 3-4 new audio clips or short verbal descriptions (e.g., "This animal is quiet and furry. It lives in the trees. It is slow.") using previously learned vocabulary. Students listen carefully and write down or draw the animal, or circle it on a multiple-choice worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of identification: Student correctly identifies animals based on audio/verbal cues (e.g., points to the sloth for "slow and furry").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates comprehension of adjectives + nouns in context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds appropriately to simple WH-questions (e.g., "What animal is it?").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket by coloring a happy face if they understood most of the animal words, and a neutral face if they need more practice. They also complete the sentence: "I can hear the word ______ (e.g., 'monkey')."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new animal word did I like to hear today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I point to the 'rainforest' picture when the teacher says it?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What listening activities were most effective for identifying vocabulary? Students responded well to the "Listen and Point" activity with flashcards and the "Guessing Game" with sounds and descriptions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? I need to ensure clear pronunciation modeling for all new words. Perhaps more TPR for the adjectives. I will prepare more individual listening checks.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: This lesson introduced key rainforest vocabulary through listening. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will use these same vocabulary words in short, simple written texts for students to recognize and understand in context.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>